@heaths Thanks for tracking down and confirming this is a timing issue. Instead of a hardcoded sleep statement I think we can add in a retry/backoff mechanism here as we do in other commands such as `repo fork`.

https://github.com/cli/cli/blob/3a228694396e52f99aceb3496da5b025bd1c067f/pkg/cmd/repo/fork/fork.go#L333

Only question I have is should we backoff/retry the code flow that calls `git clone` as well or just flow that calls `git fetch`? I am going to mark this help wanted and whomever wants to pick it up can copy the example in `repo fork`.

@samcoe, I've got a fix but it seems there's a simpler (less verbose) change and I'm wondering why `gh` doesn't just call `git.Client.Clone()` whether creating from a template or not. Here's the brunt of the modified code:

```go
		// Allow injecting alternative BackOff in tests.
		if opts.BackOff == nil {
			bo := backoff.NewConstantBackOff(3 * time.Second)
			opts.BackOff = bo
		}

		ctx := context.Background()
		err = backoff.Retry(func() error {
			var err error
			if opts.LicenseTemplate == "" && opts.GitIgnoreTemplate == "" {
				// cloning empty repository or template
				checkoutBranch := ""
				if opts.Template != "" {
					// use the template's default branch
					checkoutBranch = templateRepoMainBranch
				}
				if err = localInit(ctx, opts.GitClient, remoteURL, repo.RepoName(), checkoutBranch); err == nil {
					return err
				}
			} else if _, err = opts.GitClient.Clone(ctx, remoteURL, []string{}); err == nil {
				return err
			}

			var execError errWithExitCode
			if errors.As(err, &execError) && execError.ExitCode() == 128 {
				return err
			}
			return backoff.Permanent(err)
		}, backoff.WithContext(backoff.WithMaxRetries(opts.BackOff, 3), ctx))
```

But when we retry `localInit()` I have to account for the remote having already been added. It's not hard to do, certainly, but the branch in code seems otherwise unnecessary and harder to maintain than just cloning the template then calling `Clone()`. From a `git blame` it seems you would probably have the best context on the history of this design decision. I'm wondering if indeed it was intentional, or just a point in time thing e.g., perhaps `Clone()` hadn't yet existed and this instance was just never refactored.

